WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.596 --> 00:00:03.336
(loud whooshing)

00:00:03.336 --> 00:00:04.350
- What's up Believe Nation?

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:06.264
I started the Mentor
Me series with the goal

00:00:06.264 --> 00:00:09.358
to try to learn from people
who've done a lot more than us,

00:00:09.358 --> 00:00:11.288
hang around them a little bit longer,

00:00:11.288 --> 00:00:12.879
and by being in their environment,

00:00:12.879 --> 00:00:15.096
hopefully some of their
mindsets, their beliefs,

00:00:15.096 --> 00:00:17.509
their way of thinking, seep into us,

00:00:17.509 --> 00:00:20.612
to help us become the
best version of ourselves.

00:00:20.612 --> 00:00:22.356
So today we're going to
learn from Jim Carrey

00:00:22.356 --> 00:00:23.897
on staying motivated.

00:00:23.897 --> 00:00:24.730
Mentor me Jim.

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:26.664
And as always guys, as
you're watching the clip,

00:00:26.664 --> 00:00:29.009
if you hear something that
really really resonates with you,

00:00:29.009 --> 00:00:30.736
please leave it down in the comments below

00:00:30.736 --> 00:00:33.420
and put quotes around it so
other people can be inspired,

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:35.553
and also when you write it
down you're much more likely

00:00:35.553 --> 00:00:37.920
to lock it in for yourself too.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:38.753
Enjoy.

00:00:38.753 --> 00:00:41.586
(inspiring music)

00:00:51.936 --> 00:00:53.685
- [Narrator] Silliness
has been with Jim Carrey

00:00:53.685 --> 00:00:55.382
since he was a baby.

00:00:55.382 --> 00:00:57.704
Born, with a crooked hat.

00:00:57.704 --> 00:00:59.750
His face has never failed to get a laugh,

00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:02.426
at family parties or at
school, where he says,

00:01:02.426 --> 00:01:05.588
being the class clown
helped him to make friends.

00:01:05.588 --> 00:01:09.036
But Jim dropped out of school
in grade nine to mold his act.

00:01:09.036 --> 00:01:12.506
Two years later, his first TV appearance.

00:01:12.506 --> 00:01:15.675
The impressions were there,
but the nuances weren't.

00:01:15.675 --> 00:01:17.299
- Thank you very much.

00:01:17.299 --> 00:01:19.259
My name is Jimmy Carter.

00:01:19.259 --> 00:01:23.623
And I am the president of
the United States of America.

00:01:23.623 --> 00:01:24.456
Thank you.

00:01:27.494 --> 00:01:29.578
Thank you very much.

00:01:29.578 --> 00:01:31.685
- [Narrator] Though the
nuances are here now.

00:01:31.685 --> 00:01:33.768
They did not come easily.

00:01:35.357 --> 00:01:37.229
- And I used to be--

00:01:37.229 --> 00:01:38.307
- [Narrator] Jim struggled,

00:01:38.307 --> 00:01:40.363
trying to perfect his impressions.

00:01:40.363 --> 00:01:44.530
He usually performed at a
popular comedy club in Toronto.

00:01:45.489 --> 00:01:47.586
- I used to drive like
50 miles back and forth

00:01:47.586 --> 00:01:52.021
from Jackson's Point every
second night or every night,

00:01:52.021 --> 00:01:54.854
to do a set at Yuk Yuk's for free.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:57.173
No pay.

00:01:58.013 --> 00:01:59.081
Ooh!

00:01:59.081 --> 00:02:03.074
Not even a gas stub or anything, nothing.

00:02:03.074 --> 00:02:07.918
And uh I used to do that and
did that for the longest time

00:02:07.918 --> 00:02:10.852
just kept working until
people started to take notice,

00:02:10.852 --> 00:02:14.185
you know, and, and the rest was history.

00:02:15.378 --> 00:02:16.907
- At one point, or not at one point,

00:02:16.907 --> 00:02:18.432
'cause I hear you still do,

00:02:18.432 --> 00:02:21.206
you would be in front of a
mirror and just make faces

00:02:21.206 --> 00:02:22.039
all the time.

00:02:22.039 --> 00:02:22.872
- Yeah.

00:02:22.872 --> 00:02:25.333
For me it was so much fun
to me to sit in my room

00:02:25.333 --> 00:02:29.176
and make faces and talk to
myself in different voices

00:02:29.176 --> 00:02:31.323
and things and I'd go.

00:02:31.323 --> 00:02:33.373
Looks like Sammy.

00:02:33.373 --> 00:02:35.638
It is like Sammy you know.

00:02:35.638 --> 00:02:37.637
Yeah he does that, you know.

00:02:37.637 --> 00:02:39.094
I'd just sit for hours doing that

00:02:39.094 --> 00:02:42.075
and my parents were so
worried they would, you know,

00:02:42.075 --> 00:02:43.669
they would say, you know,
my mother tried to scare me

00:02:43.669 --> 00:02:46.021
with the spiritual thing, you know.

00:02:46.021 --> 00:02:47.197
Just like if you don't stop doing that

00:02:47.197 --> 00:02:48.774
you're going to see the devil.

00:02:48.774 --> 00:02:50.713
You know that kind of thing.

00:02:50.713 --> 00:02:54.842
And I, of course that
just made me do it more.

00:02:54.842 --> 00:02:56.705
I mean I got a lot of
support from my parents.

00:02:56.705 --> 00:02:58.938
That's the one thing that I
always appreciated was that

00:02:58.938 --> 00:03:00.601
they didn't tell me I was being stupid,

00:03:00.601 --> 00:03:02.130
they told me I was being funny.

00:03:02.130 --> 00:03:02.963
- [Interviewer] Uh huh.

00:03:02.963 --> 00:03:04.233
- That was the difference.

00:03:04.233 --> 00:03:05.563
- When you were 15

00:03:05.563 --> 00:03:09.238
your father took you to
a comedy club to perform.

00:03:09.238 --> 00:03:11.833
- My mother dresses me
in a polyester suit,

00:03:11.833 --> 00:03:14.804
tells me, you know, this is
what all the boys are wearing.

00:03:14.804 --> 00:03:17.168
And sends me down there.

00:03:17.168 --> 00:03:19.682
My father helped me write my act.

00:03:19.682 --> 00:03:22.667
And I went down to the comedy club

00:03:22.667 --> 00:03:25.430
and the owner of the club
had this little thing

00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:27.604
that he liked to do where he would

00:03:27.604 --> 00:03:29.247
stand backstage with a microphone

00:03:29.247 --> 00:03:30.943
and all kinds of fun effects.

00:03:30.943 --> 00:03:34.369
And you know, heckle new comedians.

00:03:34.369 --> 00:03:36.443
And so when I got up there
it was just horrendous,

00:03:36.443 --> 00:03:37.553
he was like backstage going,

00:03:37.553 --> 00:03:40.880
totally boring, totally
boring, totally boring.

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:42.292
They were playing, you know, excerpts from

00:03:42.292 --> 00:03:44.353
Jesus Christ Superstar like, you know,

00:03:44.353 --> 00:03:47.313
crucify him, crucify him,
crucify him, that kind of thing.

00:03:47.313 --> 00:03:50.895
So, you know, when you go through
a few of those experiences

00:03:50.895 --> 00:03:52.860
you kind of develop a thick skin

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:55.184
when it comes to being
ridiculed or whatever.

00:03:55.184 --> 00:03:56.967
- Why didn't you give it up?

00:03:56.967 --> 00:03:59.382
- I couldn't I mean, I don't have a trade.

00:03:59.382 --> 00:04:01.511
(laughing)

00:04:01.511 --> 00:04:02.813
This is my spot.

00:04:02.813 --> 00:04:06.405
This is the center of the universe.

00:04:06.405 --> 00:04:10.572
This is where I hang out
with Buddha and Krishna and,

00:04:11.644 --> 00:04:12.935
you know, all those guys.

00:04:12.935 --> 00:04:14.802
- Are you a Buddhist?
- Jesus.

00:04:14.802 --> 00:04:17.228
I'm a Buddhist, I'm a
Muslim, I'm a Christian,

00:04:17.228 --> 00:04:19.698
I'm whatever you want me to be.

00:04:19.698 --> 00:04:22.052
You know it all comes
down to the same thing.

00:04:22.052 --> 00:04:25.051
You're in a loving place or
you're in an unloving place.

00:04:25.051 --> 00:04:29.089
If you are with me right
now, you cannot be unhappy.

00:04:29.089 --> 00:04:30.832
It's not possible.

00:04:30.832 --> 00:04:32.223
It's not possible.

00:04:32.223 --> 00:04:33.193
Just try.

00:04:33.193 --> 00:04:35.231
- [Interviewer] His faith
his personal and mystical.

00:04:35.231 --> 00:04:37.330
He believes that miracles will come true,

00:04:37.330 --> 00:04:38.712
if you just believe in them.

00:04:38.712 --> 00:04:41.959
And he has his life and
his career to prove it.

00:04:41.959 --> 00:04:44.560
But he does think that people
who express strong feelings

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:48.596
about spirituality, run the
risk of being labeled a kook.

00:04:48.596 --> 00:04:50.763
- You know, what the hell.

00:04:51.743 --> 00:04:53.852
What the hell, you know, whatever.

00:04:53.852 --> 00:04:55.519
I just want to be myself.

00:04:55.519 --> 00:04:58.425
If I lose a few fringe people
who can't deal with the fact

00:04:58.425 --> 00:05:01.330
that, you know, that there's
other forces in the world,

00:05:01.330 --> 00:05:02.820
that's okay with me.

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:05.492
When I was a kid, I
brought relief to my family

00:05:05.492 --> 00:05:07.107
by taking their painful concerns

00:05:07.107 --> 00:05:09.967
and alchemizing them into comedy.

00:05:09.967 --> 00:05:13.657
And they enjoyed watching
me play, most of the time.

00:05:13.657 --> 00:05:16.154
Later my career in show business blossomed

00:05:16.154 --> 00:05:19.131
after I made the conscious
decision to serve the audience

00:05:19.131 --> 00:05:21.480
what I thought they really needed to see.

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:23.479
Someone who's carefree in the world,

00:05:23.479 --> 00:05:27.087
and treats it all with a
healthy doth of irreverenth.

00:05:27.087 --> 00:05:30.004
Yeth, thath how I meanth to say it.

00:05:31.061 --> 00:05:34.334
Since very early in my memory,
the real thrill in my life

00:05:34.334 --> 00:05:35.902
wasn't personal recognition,

00:05:35.902 --> 00:05:38.415
as much as knowing that
I could affect people

00:05:38.415 --> 00:05:41.206
in a positive way and have fun doing it.

00:05:41.206 --> 00:05:43.443
Before I had money I used
to write have a nice day

00:05:43.443 --> 00:05:46.300
on $20 bills and leave them
where I knew people in need

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:47.823
would find them.

00:05:47.823 --> 00:05:49.402
I didn't wait around to see their smiles

00:05:49.402 --> 00:05:52.978
I just was satisfied knowing
that for one brief moment

00:05:52.978 --> 00:05:55.564
they might feel a little bit lucky.

00:05:55.564 --> 00:05:58.992
As time passed my interest in
people's welfare has grown,

00:05:58.992 --> 00:06:02.760
but for me living in this
world will always be a game,

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:04.338
the object of which is to create

00:06:04.338 --> 00:06:06.820
as much goodwill as possible.

00:06:06.820 --> 00:06:08.584
I realize now, more than ever,

00:06:08.584 --> 00:06:10.957
that the most valuable commodity in life,

00:06:10.957 --> 00:06:13.610
is the feeling you get when you give.

00:06:13.610 --> 00:06:17.242
Compassion is the currency
that leads to true wealth.

00:06:17.242 --> 00:06:19.673
I created Better U so I
could continue to expand

00:06:19.673 --> 00:06:22.054
my awareness of what people need,

00:06:22.054 --> 00:06:25.521
to relieve or prevent
suffering wherever possible.

00:06:25.521 --> 00:06:28.520
And to offer a kind of
assistance that allows people

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:32.876
to grow towards independence
and self-actualization.

00:06:32.876 --> 00:06:35.806
And a healthy doth of irrevernth.

00:06:35.806 --> 00:06:38.947
A Better U means a better universe.

00:06:38.947 --> 00:06:41.224
Anything that doesn't work out.

00:06:41.224 --> 00:06:42.950
Any show that I might not get.

00:06:42.950 --> 00:06:47.335
Any show that I might do
that doesn't turn out well

00:06:47.335 --> 00:06:49.611
and gets pulled off or
something like that,

00:06:49.611 --> 00:06:53.742
I always think of as this is
the way it was supposed to be,

00:06:53.742 --> 00:06:55.752
that's the way it was supposed to happen,

00:06:55.752 --> 00:06:58.397
because there's something
bigger down the road.

00:06:58.397 --> 00:07:00.121
There's something better down the road

00:07:00.121 --> 00:07:03.996
and this is happening because
I'm not ready or, you know,

00:07:03.996 --> 00:07:05.522
and it's always happened that way.

00:07:05.522 --> 00:07:07.502
I mean the first time I
ever went to Yuk Yuk's

00:07:07.502 --> 00:07:10.970
when I was 15 years old,
I went to Church Street.

00:07:10.970 --> 00:07:14.081
The old Yuk Yuk's it was two
lanes of a bowling alley.

00:07:14.081 --> 00:07:17.081
And I went there and I was not good.

00:07:18.540 --> 00:07:19.470
(laughing)

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:20.836
I was all right, I mean I was--

00:07:20.836 --> 00:07:21.676
- You were 15.

00:07:21.676 --> 00:07:24.277
- I was 15, I had a
white polyester suit on,

00:07:24.277 --> 00:07:26.845
my mum put on me and I
went down there with Dad

00:07:26.845 --> 00:07:31.612
and got up on stage and I
was doing all right really.

00:07:31.612 --> 00:07:34.579
But the owner of the club
got on the mike backstage

00:07:34.579 --> 00:07:36.728
and started going totally boring,

00:07:36.728 --> 00:07:38.705
and all of sudden it
wasn't hip enough for them,

00:07:38.705 --> 00:07:41.633
so I was pretty shattered
and I didn't go back

00:07:41.633 --> 00:07:43.150
for two years.

00:07:43.150 --> 00:07:45.112
And then my father said, when I was 17,

00:07:45.112 --> 00:07:48.005
I started doing little
shows here and there again.

00:07:48.005 --> 00:07:50.255
And he said when don't
you go back to Yuk Yuk's.

00:07:50.255 --> 00:07:53.011
I said, no way, I don't
want to go back there.

00:07:53.011 --> 00:07:55.821
And he said well you really, you know,

00:07:55.821 --> 00:07:57.230
you should get over it.

00:07:57.230 --> 00:08:00.267
I mean if you're going
to go into the business

00:08:00.267 --> 00:08:01.907
and you want to do it seriously

00:08:01.907 --> 00:08:04.262
you can't have those barriers, you know.

00:08:04.262 --> 00:08:08.043
And I went back and I did
great and I became a regular

00:08:08.043 --> 00:08:10.399
and a headliner and it built from there,

00:08:10.399 --> 00:08:12.168
and then when I went down to Los Angeles

00:08:12.168 --> 00:08:13.748
the same thing happened.

00:08:13.748 --> 00:08:16.306
I went to the Comedy
Store, I did all right,

00:08:16.306 --> 00:08:18.588
but not good enough to become a regular.

00:08:18.588 --> 00:08:22.082
And I became totally discouraged
with the Comedy Store.

00:08:22.082 --> 00:08:23.058
I came back to Toronto

00:08:23.058 --> 00:08:24.846
like everybody who
doesn't do well down there

00:08:24.846 --> 00:08:27.947
comes back going, I hate Los Angeles.

00:08:27.947 --> 00:08:30.050
It's such a crummy place.

00:08:30.050 --> 00:08:33.129
No really, you can't raise
kids there, and all that stuff.

00:08:33.129 --> 00:08:35.526
And, Comedy Store is a
rat race, it's a rat race.

00:08:35.526 --> 00:08:36.578
(laughing)

00:08:36.578 --> 00:08:40.997
But then two years later,
again, my father, he says to me,

00:08:40.997 --> 00:08:43.143
well you should try and
get in to the Comedy Store.

00:08:43.143 --> 00:08:46.393
And my manager set up a showcase there,

00:08:47.909 --> 00:08:51.361
I went in and I did well
and became a regular there,

00:08:51.361 --> 00:08:53.778
and everything has
happened from there since.

00:08:53.778 --> 00:08:55.366
It's just, it's been incredible,

00:08:55.366 --> 00:08:57.418
and it always goes in stages like that.

00:08:57.418 --> 00:09:00.051
So I accept the fact
that I fail, you know,

00:09:00.051 --> 00:09:02.735
because I know that it's, that's the way

00:09:02.735 --> 00:09:03.906
it's supposed to be.

00:09:03.906 --> 00:09:05.219
I have no education.

00:09:05.219 --> 00:09:07.314
I had no high school diploma.

00:09:07.314 --> 00:09:09.630
I didn't even have a GED.

00:09:09.630 --> 00:09:13.569
I quit high school and,
halfway through grade ten.

00:09:13.569 --> 00:09:14.838
I didn't have a lot of options.

00:09:14.838 --> 00:09:17.671
I would be a dishwasher, a waiter.

00:09:19.004 --> 00:09:20.693
Custodial work of some sort.

00:09:20.693 --> 00:09:25.029
So I decided I might as well
go and do something creative.

00:09:25.029 --> 00:09:27.949
I decided to go to the Comedy
Store, the Laugh Factory

00:09:27.949 --> 00:09:30.444
and the improv, you know,

00:09:30.444 --> 00:09:33.565
all of those clubs every night,

00:09:33.565 --> 00:09:36.398
and make my 25 bucks a pop, a set.

00:09:38.691 --> 00:09:41.676
It was my version of being a waiter.

00:09:41.676 --> 00:09:44.778
It was a compromise I decided to make.

00:09:44.778 --> 00:09:47.578
Out of that came In Living Color,

00:09:47.578 --> 00:09:51.745
and out of that came touring
auditoriums, 5000 seaters.

00:09:52.896 --> 00:09:55.531
It was a really creative time.

00:09:55.531 --> 00:09:56.364
I was able to prove

00:09:56.364 --> 00:09:58.535
that I didn't have to be an impressionist.

00:09:58.535 --> 00:10:01.308
That I had a personality of my own and,

00:10:01.308 --> 00:10:03.949
however abrasive it might
be from time to time.

00:10:03.949 --> 00:10:06.827
But long story short, all of
it came from that compromise,

00:10:06.827 --> 00:10:11.171
from that deciding to do
what I didn't want to do.

00:10:11.171 --> 00:10:12.838
Things you can't do,

00:10:13.876 --> 00:10:15.672
or the things you're not allowed to do.

00:10:15.672 --> 00:10:18.501
Like if the executives come
and say you can't do that,

00:10:18.501 --> 00:10:21.456
whatever it is, it's too
expensive, you get angry,

00:10:21.456 --> 00:10:23.016
you get out of your mind about it,

00:10:23.016 --> 00:10:25.920
because you've been clinging
to this concept and this image

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:29.244
that you have of how it should be done.

00:10:29.244 --> 00:10:31.161
Invariably, most times,

00:10:33.021 --> 00:10:35.381
after that storm has passed,

00:10:35.381 --> 00:10:37.245
and you actually sit down and go okay,

00:10:37.245 --> 00:10:39.608
we can't do it that way, how can we do it,

00:10:39.608 --> 00:10:42.482
you end up with something even better

00:10:42.482 --> 00:10:44.188
than what was there before.

00:10:44.188 --> 00:10:48.233
And you go, that's the
universe, that's how it works.

00:10:48.233 --> 00:10:50.569
Just like relationships, like everything.

00:10:50.569 --> 00:10:54.127
The thing that is taken away,
that you're clinging to,

00:10:54.127 --> 00:10:56.868
you're trying to fix,
you're trying to secure it,

00:10:56.868 --> 00:10:58.730
make sure it doesn't go away.

00:10:58.730 --> 00:11:00.580
You think that your
life will be destroyed,

00:11:00.580 --> 00:11:02.536
everything will be ruined.

00:11:02.536 --> 00:11:04.496
And it's just the universe going no,

00:11:04.496 --> 00:11:06.178
I have something better for you.

00:11:06.178 --> 00:11:07.665
Just have faith.

00:11:07.665 --> 00:11:10.207
Sometimes compromise is actually the way

00:11:10.207 --> 00:11:11.824
to get to what you want.

00:11:11.824 --> 00:11:16.820
Doing what you don't want is
the way to what you do want.

00:11:16.820 --> 00:11:17.952
On occasion.

00:11:17.952 --> 00:11:20.722
I would never have made it
without going back and doing

00:11:20.722 --> 00:11:22.150
what I didn't want to do.

00:11:22.150 --> 00:11:24.375
- Jim I wanted to ask you, some years ago,

00:11:24.375 --> 00:11:26.649
you wrote yourself a check.

00:11:26.649 --> 00:11:28.333
- Yes I did.

00:11:28.333 --> 00:11:31.688
I guess it's like six,
seven years ago now.

00:11:31.688 --> 00:11:35.320
I wrote a check out to
myself for $10 million

00:11:35.320 --> 00:11:37.820
for acting services rendered--

00:11:38.834 --> 00:11:40.801
- [Interviewer] In 1995.

00:11:40.801 --> 00:11:42.718
- By Thanksgiving 1995.

00:11:44.005 --> 00:11:46.242
And I put it in my wallet and I carried it

00:11:46.242 --> 00:11:47.909
for that many years.

00:11:50.134 --> 00:11:54.829
And what happened was
basically, a few months ago,

00:11:54.829 --> 00:11:57.813
we did the deal for the
The Mask for $10 million.

00:11:57.813 --> 00:12:00.172
- $10 million payment for doing The Mask.

00:12:00.172 --> 00:12:01.460
- Right.

00:12:01.460 --> 00:12:03.303
Payment for doing The Mask too.

00:12:03.303 --> 00:12:07.053
And so it's very surreal
for me it's strange.

00:12:08.141 --> 00:12:10.161
And I used to go up to
Mulholland Drive every night,

00:12:10.161 --> 00:12:12.755
sit on the side of the road,
look out at the lights,

00:12:12.755 --> 00:12:15.922
and go, you know, I'm a popular actor.

00:12:16.930 --> 00:12:20.516
I'm a, you know, every
director wants to work with me.

00:12:20.516 --> 00:12:23.000
And I used to like open my
arms and imagine things,

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:25.868
you know, things that I wanted
coming my way and stuff.

00:12:25.868 --> 00:12:29.914
And what I would do, it was a
way of dealing with, you know,

00:12:29.914 --> 00:12:32.650
being completely out of work.

00:12:32.650 --> 00:12:35.080
I would stay up there
until I actually believed

00:12:35.080 --> 00:12:36.563
that I had all these things.

00:12:36.563 --> 00:12:38.813
And then I would drive down
the mountain feeling like

00:12:38.813 --> 00:12:40.963
the biggest star in Hollywood.

00:12:40.963 --> 00:12:42.555
Like I was working all the time,

00:12:42.555 --> 00:12:45.489
like the best people
wanted to work with me.

00:12:45.489 --> 00:12:47.021
And I had my thing in my pocket,

00:12:47.021 --> 00:12:48.863
my little check in my pocket.

00:12:48.863 --> 00:12:51.027
You know it's our intention.

00:12:51.027 --> 00:12:53.253
Our intention is everything.

00:12:53.253 --> 00:12:55.705
Nothing happens on this planet without it.

00:12:55.705 --> 00:12:58.507
Not one single thing has
ever been accomplished

00:12:58.507 --> 00:13:00.697
without intention.

00:13:00.697 --> 00:13:03.185
So I started thinking about my life

00:13:03.185 --> 00:13:05.322
and I started thinking
about this conference,

00:13:05.322 --> 00:13:09.285
and what we're about and I
looked back and I thought, well,

00:13:09.285 --> 00:13:10.593
I was two people.

00:13:10.593 --> 00:13:13.758
My whole life I was in the
living room entertaining people,

00:13:13.758 --> 00:13:14.591
being a monkey.

00:13:14.591 --> 00:13:15.841
(making monkey noises)

00:13:15.841 --> 00:13:19.277
You know doing my thing
for the company and,

00:13:19.277 --> 00:13:22.457
and trying to relieve my
mother who was suffering.

00:13:22.457 --> 00:13:25.701
She had rheumatoid
arthritis and phlebitis,

00:13:25.701 --> 00:13:29.314
and everything under the
sun that was nagging at her,

00:13:29.314 --> 00:13:33.481
and she was depressed, and
I wanted her to be free.

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:37.547
And I wanted her to realize that

00:13:39.522 --> 00:13:40.992
her life was worth something

00:13:40.992 --> 00:13:45.159
'cause she'd gave birth to
someone who's worth something.

00:13:47.002 --> 00:13:51.002
And then I would go into
my room and I would sit

00:13:54.177 --> 00:13:55.852
with a legal pad.

00:13:55.852 --> 00:13:56.685
(laughing)

00:13:56.685 --> 00:13:57.905
I was a little kid.

00:13:57.905 --> 00:14:00.759
And I would sit there and
I would try to figure out

00:14:00.759 --> 00:14:03.517
what it meant, what it was all about.

00:14:03.517 --> 00:14:05.257
Why are we here?

00:14:05.257 --> 00:14:06.340
What is this?

00:14:07.803 --> 00:14:11.970
And one day I read something
from Buddha that said

00:14:13.196 --> 00:14:17.363
that all spirituality is
about relieving suffering.

00:14:18.899 --> 00:14:21.209
And I suddenly realized,

00:14:21.209 --> 00:14:23.026
that's what I'm doing in the other room.

00:14:23.026 --> 00:14:24.987
(laughing)

00:14:24.987 --> 00:14:26.320
And I'm aligned.

00:14:29.199 --> 00:14:32.875
You know this, my purpose
is aligned with this.

00:14:32.875 --> 00:14:35.682
So I felt incredibly lucky.

00:14:35.682 --> 00:14:37.619
I lose sight of that all the time.

00:14:37.619 --> 00:14:41.989
I get caught up in different
concerns and ego concerns.

00:14:41.989 --> 00:14:45.452
But I'm so lucky to be
a part of this community

00:14:45.452 --> 00:14:48.535
and to do something that is of value.

00:14:49.595 --> 00:14:54.464
And I really cherish that
and we are all one thing,

00:14:54.464 --> 00:14:55.791
it really is true.

00:14:55.791 --> 00:14:59.417
Now fear is going to be
a player in your life.

00:14:59.417 --> 00:15:01.788
But you get to decide how much.

00:15:01.788 --> 00:15:04.174
You can spend your whole
life imaging ghosts,

00:15:04.174 --> 00:15:06.602
worrying about the pathway to the future.

00:15:06.602 --> 00:15:10.538
But all there will ever be
is what's happening here,

00:15:10.538 --> 00:15:12.801
and the decisions we make in this moment,

00:15:12.801 --> 00:15:16.049
which are based in either love or fear.

00:15:16.049 --> 00:15:18.344
So many of us choose our path out of fear

00:15:18.344 --> 00:15:20.511
disguised as practicality.

00:15:22.342 --> 00:15:24.644
What we really want seems
impossibly out of reach

00:15:24.644 --> 00:15:27.131
and ridiculous to expect
so we never dare to ask

00:15:27.131 --> 00:15:28.296
the universe for it.

00:15:28.296 --> 00:15:31.126
I'm saying, I'm the proof

00:15:31.126 --> 00:15:33.875
that you can ask the universe for it.

00:15:33.875 --> 00:15:35.711
My father could've been a great comedian

00:15:35.711 --> 00:15:38.196
but he didn't believe that
that was possible for him.

00:15:38.196 --> 00:15:40.516
And so he made a conservative choice.

00:15:40.516 --> 00:15:43.169
Instead he got a safe
job as an accountant.

00:15:43.169 --> 00:15:45.230
And when I was 12 years old

00:15:45.230 --> 00:15:47.520
he was let go from that safe job,

00:15:47.520 --> 00:15:50.435
and our family had to do
whatever we could to survive.

00:15:50.435 --> 00:15:52.907
I learned many great
lessons from my father,

00:15:52.907 --> 00:15:55.306
not the least of which
was that you can fail

00:15:55.306 --> 00:15:58.490
at what you don't want, so you
might as well take a chance

00:15:58.490 --> 00:15:59.781
on doing what you love.

00:15:59.781 --> 00:16:02.901
(cheering and applauding)

00:16:02.901 --> 00:16:04.252
- So thank you guys so much for watching.

00:16:04.252 --> 00:16:06.665
I'd love to know what did
you learn from this video?

00:16:06.665 --> 00:16:08.804
What clips spoke to you the most,

00:16:08.804 --> 00:16:10.906
and what are going to
take from these clips

00:16:10.906 --> 00:16:12.982
and immediately apply to your life

00:16:12.982 --> 00:16:14.499
or to your business somehow.

00:16:14.499 --> 00:16:15.903
Leave it down in the comments below,

00:16:15.903 --> 00:16:17.446
I'm super-curious to find out.

00:16:17.446 --> 00:16:19.448
Finally I want to give a quick
shout-out to Inside Junky.

00:16:19.448 --> 00:16:21.774
Thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:16:21.774 --> 00:16:23.828
Your One Word, and making
that awesome YouTube video

00:16:23.828 --> 00:16:25.070
on it as well.

00:16:25.070 --> 00:16:26.888
I'm really really glad
that you enjoyed the book

00:16:26.888 --> 00:16:28.484
and I appreciate your support.

00:16:28.484 --> 00:16:30.837
- [Narrator] If you knew
how to start your business,

00:16:30.837 --> 00:16:33.868
or how to make more money, this
book may just be the answer

00:16:33.868 --> 00:16:35.284
that you're looking for.

00:16:35.284 --> 00:16:39.111
Today we're talking about Your
One Word by Evan Carmichael.

00:16:39.111 --> 00:16:41.075
- So thank you guys so much for watching.

00:16:41.075 --> 00:16:44.268
I believe in you, I hope you
continue to believe in yourself

00:16:44.268 --> 00:16:47.595
and whatever your one word is, much love.

00:16:47.595 --> 00:16:49.010
I'll see you soon.

00:16:49.010 --> 00:16:51.341
(loud whooshing)

